	array(
		'question_text'  => 'A therapist modifies written treatment materials by using clearer language and culturally relevant examples while continuing to provide a complete assessment and informed-consent process.Which statement is most accurate?',
		'option_a'       => 'The therapist is lowering the professional standard of care',
		'option_b'       => 'The therapist is practicing outside the scope of psychotherapy',
		'option_c'       => 'The therapist should use identical materials for every client to ensure fairness',
		'option_d'       => 'The therapist is appropriately adapting how care is delivered while maintaining professional responsibilities',
		'correct_option' => 'd',
		'explanation'    => 'D. Correct.
Culturally responsive care may require modifying language, examples, pacing, communication style, or materials so that treatment is understandable and relevant to the client.
The therapist is not eliminating important information or reducing the quality of care. The professional standards involving assessment, informed consent, competence, confidentiality, and client safety remain intact.
Why the Other Answers Are Incorrect
A. Incorrect. Adaptation is not the same as providing inferior care. The therapist is improving access and understanding while preserving the necessary clinical content.
B. Incorrect. Modifying communication materials remains within ordinary clinical practice when done competently.
C. Incorrect. Identical delivery may fail to provide meaningful access. Fairness may require individualized support rather than mechanical sameness.
CTA Exam Strategy: Remember:Change the method—not the professional responsibility.',
