	array(
		'question_text'  => 'A therapist begins work with a client whose cultural and spiritual practices are unfamiliar and must respond without assuming expertise or transferring responsibility to the client. Which statement best reflects California’s treatment of cultural misunderstandings?',
		'option_a'       => 'Every cultural mistake automatically constitutes gross negligence',
		'option_b'       => 'A misunderstanding becomes lawful whenever the therapist apologizes',
		'option_c'       => 'Cultural concerns have no relevance to professional discipline',
		'option_d'       => 'A cultural misunderstanding is not automatically a statutory violation, but it may become professionally significant when it contributes to incompetence, gross negligence, deception, or reckless harm',
		'correct_option' => 'd',
		'explanation'    => 'D. Correct.
California does not classify every cultural misunderstanding or incorrect assumption as a separate statutory violation. Many mistakes can be addressed through acknowledgment, correction, education, consultation, and repair.
The concern becomes more serious when the therapist repeatedly disregards relevant information, practices beyond competence, provides grossly inadequate care, acts dishonestly, or intentionally or recklessly harms the client.
Why the Other Answers Are Incorrect
A. Incorrect. This answer is too absolute. A correctable misunderstanding does not automatically establish gross negligence.
B. Incorrect. An apology may support repair, but it does not automatically resolve incompetence, serious harm, or repeated misconduct.
C. Incorrect. Cultural issues may affect assessment, diagnosis, communication, treatment, access, and client welfare. When those effects become serious, they may contribute to disciplinary concerns.
CTA Exam Strategy: Distinguish between:Mistake recognized and repairedRepeated disregard or harmful incompetenceDo not automatically classify every cultural error as illegal—but do not minimize conduct that causes serious harm.',
