	array(
		'question_text'  => 'A therapist recognizes that an automatic assumption may have influenced assessment and must decide how to examine and correct the effect on care. Which statement best reflects the clinician’s ethical responsibility regarding bias?',
		'option_a'       => 'A biased thought automatically constitutes unprofessional conduct',
		'option_b'       => 'Clinicians can eliminate all bias through sufficient training',
		'option_c'       => 'Bias becomes a professional concern when it affects judgment or conduct without adequate reflection, evidence, or correction',
		'option_d'       => 'Implicit bias is harmless because the clinician may not consciously recognize it',
		'correct_option' => 'c',
		'explanation'    => 'C. Correct.
Clinicians may experience automatic reactions, assumptions, or preferences. The existence of an internal reaction does not by itself establish unethical conduct.
The professional concern arises when bias influences assessment, diagnosis, risk evaluation, treatment, documentation, referral, or access to services without sufficient evidence or corrective action. Clinicians remain responsible for examining how their reactions may affect client welfare.
Why the Other Answers Are Incorrect
A. Incorrect. A biased thought is not automatically equivalent to misconduct. The clinician’s responsibility is to recognize, manage, and prevent the reaction from distorting professional decisions.
B. Incorrect. Training can reduce blind spots and improve practice, but it does not guarantee that a clinician will become completely free from bias. Ongoing self-reflection and evidence review remain necessary.
D. Incorrect. Lack of conscious awareness does not make the effect harmless. Implicit bias may subtly influence credibility judgments, diagnosis, documentation, and treatment recommendations.
CTA Exam Strategy: Remember:A thought requires awareness. A professional decision requires evidence.',
